1
00:00:06,206 --> 00:00:08,375
[rock 'n' roll music playing]

2
00:00:16,249 --> 00:00:17,951
[engine revving]

3
00:00:20,266 --> 00:00:21,466
[tires screeching]

4
00:00:26,666 --> 00:00:31,133
[Jerry] This is a 1970 
Plymouth Road Runner Superbird.

5
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:33,933
Listen to all these great words
that describe it.

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:35,066
[engine revving]

7
00:00:35,133 --> 00:00:39,400
A 440-cubic inch six pack
with three Holley two-barrel carbs.

8
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:43,100
What is that? You don't know,
but it sounds fantastic.

9
00:00:43,166 --> 00:00:44,233
[upbeat music playing]

10
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:47,766
It's got Mopar's legendary
TorqueFlite 727 automatic

11
00:00:47,833 --> 00:00:49,699
with a 355 rear end.

12
00:00:51,433 --> 00:00:55,033
The front nose cone 
adds 19 inches to the overall length,

13
00:00:55,099 --> 00:00:57,866
and the rear wing stands five feet tall.

14
00:00:59,666 --> 00:01:02,033
Okay, so the story 
with this crazy thing is

15
00:01:02,099 --> 00:01:06,666
Plymouth built this to try and convince
Richard Petty, the famous race car driver,

16
00:01:06,766 --> 00:01:09,000
to race for them in 1970.

17
00:01:09,066 --> 00:01:11,600
He saw the car and went, "I'm in."

18
00:01:12,266 --> 00:01:17,033
These cars could go over 200 miles an hour
and won tons of races.

19
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:19,033
[jazz music playing]

20
00:01:19,633 --> 00:01:23,433
I remember as a kid seeing the
Road Runner cartoon character on the side

21
00:01:23,533 --> 00:01:26,333
and thinking, "This is fun, this is crazy.

22
00:01:26,433 --> 00:01:28,766
What other car is based
on a cartoon character?"

23
00:01:28,866 --> 00:01:31,466
It's happy, it's silly.
Listen to the horn.

24
00:01:31,533 --> 00:01:32,366
[beeps]

25
00:01:32,433 --> 00:01:33,633
[Jerry] That's the Road Runner!

26
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:36,366
Which is why this is the perfect car 

27
00:01:36,466 --> 00:01:41,466
for my happy, fun, unbelievably hilarious 
special guest today,

28
00:01:41,533 --> 00:01:43,333
Mr. Will Ferrell.

29
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:45,033
[phone ringing]

30
00:01:45,805 --> 00:01:47,073
-[Jerry] Will?
-[Will] Yeah?

31
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:48,842
-It's Jerry.
-[Will] Of course.

32
00:01:48,908 --> 00:01:50,310
[Jerry] Jerry is a comedy name.

33
00:01:50,376 --> 00:01:53,279
Whereas, your first name
just comes to a dead stop.

34
00:01:53,346 --> 00:01:54,914
[Will] Otherwise, it's a juxtaposition.

35
00:01:54,981 --> 00:01:56,883
"How could that guy be funny?
His name's Will."

36
00:01:57,433 --> 00:01:59,933
[Jerry] I think that's your whole career,
if I may sum it up.

37
00:02:00,053 --> 00:02:03,189
-You just don't act like you look.
-[Will] Yep, that's it.

38
00:02:03,256 --> 00:02:05,191
[Jerry]
You have worked the hell outta that.

39
00:02:05,258 --> 00:02:06,259
[Will] You've nailed it.

40
00:02:07,127 --> 00:02:09,496
-Where do I pick you up?
-Pick me up... I'm at the park.

41
00:02:09,566 --> 00:02:11,066
[Jerry] The park? What kind of park?

42
00:02:11,166 --> 00:02:13,066
[Will] You'll see me. 
I'll be on the swing set.

43
00:02:17,170 --> 00:02:21,474
Hi, I'm Jerry Seinfeld, and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

44
00:02:22,633 --> 00:02:23,500
[car honks]

45
00:02:24,133 --> 00:02:25,633
-[Jerry] Hey, little boy.
-[Will] Hey.

46
00:02:25,733 --> 00:02:26,966
You like candy?

47
00:02:27,066 --> 00:02:29,133
Yeah. I'm getting out of here.

48
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:30,600
[slow music playing]

49
00:02:32,033 --> 00:02:35,833
I had a school interview
with my oldest boy today.

50
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:36,833
-Really?
-Yeah.

51
00:02:36,900 --> 00:02:38,133
He's in sixth grade.

52
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:40,000
-He's such a good politician.
-Really?

53
00:02:40,066 --> 00:02:41,633
Oh, he will talk about,

54
00:02:41,733 --> 00:02:45,066
"I like the inclusive nature
of your school."

55
00:02:45,133 --> 00:02:47,033
I'm like, "Who is this kid?"

56
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:49,133
"I like what your mission statement says."

57
00:02:49,333 --> 00:02:50,366
[laughing]

58
00:02:50,666 --> 00:02:53,100
Which... In sixth grade,
I wasn't saying things like that.

59
00:02:53,166 --> 00:02:54,166
-No.
-Yeah.

60
00:02:54,207 --> 00:02:55,408
[inaudible]

61
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:00,733
I should've had, like, a cigar,
that I keep ignoring them when they say,

62
00:03:00,799 --> 00:03:02,266
"You can't smoke in here."

63
00:03:03,216 --> 00:03:05,185
"Oh, I didn't hear you."

64
00:03:05,266 --> 00:03:07,500
And then, let 30 seconds pass, and then...

65
00:03:08,733 --> 00:03:11,833
"So, what kind of sports teams
do you have here?"

66
00:03:11,891 --> 00:03:15,995
"Mr. Ferrell, I'm gonna ask you
for the last time to put the cigar out."

67
00:03:16,062 --> 00:03:17,697
[upbeat music playing]

68
00:03:19,899 --> 00:03:21,568
-[Will] This is great.
-[Jerry] Fun, right?

69
00:03:21,634 --> 00:03:23,269
-Cars don't smell like this anymore.
-[Jerry] No.

70
00:03:23,366 --> 00:03:25,133
They don't look like this
or feel like this.

71
00:03:25,233 --> 00:03:27,399
[Will] Did you know that
this is my color too?

72
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:29,509
-I love this color blue.
-You're kidding.

73
00:03:29,866 --> 00:03:34,200
Let's do the scale of pretentious
of these words.

74
00:03:34,533 --> 00:03:38,033
-Film, picture, movie.
-Oh.

75
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:39,566
Most pretentious, "picture."

76
00:03:39,666 --> 00:03:41,266
"It's a wonderful picture."

77
00:03:41,333 --> 00:03:43,533
It's very self-consciously hip, I think,

78
00:03:43,633 --> 00:03:45,966
-to call it a picture.
-"Film" is close second.

79
00:03:46,033 --> 00:03:47,100
"Film" is close second.

80
00:03:47,166 --> 00:03:49,533
And "movie" is undeniably
the least pretentious.

81
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:51,000
Yeah, you're right. You're right.

82
00:03:51,899 --> 00:03:56,266
Why do people in movies, film,
or pictures...

83
00:03:56,333 --> 00:03:59,200
-Right.
-...think they're very, very important?

84
00:03:59,266 --> 00:04:01,366
I don't know, but I love it so much.

85
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:03,666
[Jerry] Why the full names of the actors?

86
00:04:03,766 --> 00:04:05,100
Bradley Cooper.

87
00:04:05,166 --> 00:04:07,866
No one's calling him "Bradley"
in his life, come on.

88
00:04:07,966 --> 00:04:09,299
-Robert De Niro.
-Robert De Niro.

89
00:04:09,366 --> 00:04:10,733
But no one's calling him "Robert."

90
00:04:10,799 --> 00:04:12,133
-He's Bob.
-Right.

91
00:04:12,200 --> 00:04:13,299
-Everybody knows.
-Right.

92
00:04:13,366 --> 00:04:14,799
-I don't know him.
-Yeah. Right.

93
00:04:14,866 --> 00:04:15,933
He's gotta be Bob.

94
00:04:16,856 --> 00:04:18,458
[Jerry] What movies have you seen lately?

95
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:20,633
-I don't see a lot of things.
-You don't?

96
00:04:20,733 --> 00:04:22,233
-I gotta be honest.
-Why?

97
00:04:22,666 --> 00:04:23,599
I don't know.

98
00:04:23,666 --> 00:04:26,366
In fact, name a movie,
and I bet you I haven't seen it.

99
00:04:26,466 --> 00:04:27,833
-Whiplash.
-No.

100
00:04:27,933 --> 00:04:29,733
-Fruitvale Station.
-No.

101
00:04:29,833 --> 00:04:32,466
Tom Hanks in the hijack boat movie.

102
00:04:32,533 --> 00:04:33,966
-Captain Phillips.
-Captain Phillips.

103
00:04:34,066 --> 00:04:36,566
-Never saw it.
-Are you kidding?

104
00:04:36,633 --> 00:04:37,533
I am serious.

105
00:04:37,833 --> 00:04:39,033
Gravity. Yes.

106
00:04:39,099 --> 00:04:40,400
-No.
-Get out.

107
00:04:40,466 --> 00:04:41,766
I haven't seen it.

108
00:04:41,866 --> 00:04:43,066
That's great.

109
00:04:43,166 --> 00:04:45,566
I would love to be the one guy
that didn't see Gravity.

110
00:04:45,666 --> 00:04:46,700
I... [laughs]

111
00:04:48,666 --> 00:04:52,800
I was there
the night you opened the Irvine Improv.

112
00:04:52,892 --> 00:04:54,427
What year would that have been?

113
00:04:54,494 --> 00:04:55,695
-Do you recall?
-'85?

114
00:04:55,766 --> 00:04:58,700
-Four?
-I would've been still in high school.

115
00:04:58,766 --> 00:04:59,866
-Really?
-Yeah.

116
00:04:59,966 --> 00:05:02,933
Yeah, I remember your joke about
the moose getting picked up.

117
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:04,300
-Yes.
-And the moose is like...

118
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:06,099
Right. It was a moose that got rescued

119
00:05:06,166 --> 00:05:07,166
-from an island.
-Right.

120
00:05:07,233 --> 00:05:09,066
They picked him up with a helicopter.

121
00:05:09,166 --> 00:05:12,266
-And you were wondering--
-He was in a harness and he was floating

122
00:05:12,333 --> 00:05:13,566
and he's in midair.

123
00:05:13,666 --> 00:05:16,433
The line was,
"Well, I guess I can fly now."

124
00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:18,200
[laughs]

125
00:05:19,786 --> 00:05:21,921
-This is the California you gotta love.
-So nice.

126
00:05:21,988 --> 00:05:23,523
-This is what you dream about.
-Yeah.

127
00:05:23,656 --> 00:05:25,091
[surfer music playing]

128
00:05:26,693 --> 00:05:30,263
-L.A. beaches are so beautiful.
-Until it rains.

129
00:05:30,333 --> 00:05:32,466
And then all the sewage dumps out there.

130
00:05:32,533 --> 00:05:33,833
Could you explain...

131
00:05:34,566 --> 00:05:35,500
Welcome.

132
00:05:35,900 --> 00:05:37,500
[Jerry] You know, I've heard this.

133
00:05:37,599 --> 00:05:39,800
I have a vague understanding of,

134
00:05:39,866 --> 00:05:42,133
-when it rains, what happens?
-Yeah.

135
00:05:42,375 --> 00:05:47,180
[Will] So, L.A. was built in such a way
that the treatment facilities overflow.

136
00:05:47,513 --> 00:05:50,116
And it flows out into 
the Santa Monica Bay.

137
00:05:50,216 --> 00:05:51,784
[both laughing]

138
00:05:52,599 --> 00:05:53,566
Hungry?

139
00:05:54,599 --> 00:05:56,566
[jazz music playing]

140
00:06:00,460 --> 00:06:01,861
-He's gonna have--
-Do you have--

141
00:06:01,928 --> 00:06:03,596
-I'm gonna have decaf.
-Okay.

142
00:06:03,666 --> 00:06:04,933
And I'll have regular.

143
00:06:05,298 --> 00:06:06,966
Can I get you guys anything to drink?

144
00:06:07,967 --> 00:06:09,235
-[Will] Oh.
-Uh...

145
00:06:09,302 --> 00:06:11,904
-[stammers] I think decaf coffee?
-Could we...

146
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:13,366
Yeah? Decaf and a regular?

147
00:06:13,406 --> 00:06:14,841
-Yeah. Thank you.
-Okay. Sounds good.

148
00:06:16,833 --> 00:06:18,133
-Was that...
-That was a test.

149
00:06:19,766 --> 00:06:22,933
I like a place with
a lot of items on the menu

150
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,799
'cause you know
they do them all beautifully.

151
00:06:29,222 --> 00:06:31,657
-What do you weigh now?
-I'm like 218.

152
00:06:32,666 --> 00:06:34,400
Sometimes I'm like... I do think,

153
00:06:34,799 --> 00:06:38,900
"For the amount that I'm working out,
why am I not losing weight?"

154
00:06:39,133 --> 00:06:40,866
What would you like to look like?

155
00:06:41,267 --> 00:06:42,368
[sighs]

156
00:06:48,566 --> 00:06:51,133
-My dream would be 200.
-Is that sensitive? Am I--

157
00:06:51,666 --> 00:06:53,400
-Two hundred?
-But you know what the sad...

158
00:06:53,466 --> 00:06:56,166
I have to work out just to look fat.

159
00:06:56,700 --> 00:06:57,633
That's the problem.

160
00:06:57,700 --> 00:07:00,366
If I took my shirt off, you'd go,
"Ooh, not so good."

161
00:07:00,433 --> 00:07:01,866
That's why we have shirts.

162
00:07:01,933 --> 00:07:03,099
[guitar music playing]

163
00:07:04,433 --> 00:07:06,299
I was a bank teller for a little while.

164
00:07:06,700 --> 00:07:08,533
-In real life?
-In real life.

165
00:07:08,966 --> 00:07:14,633
I would recruit people at the bank
to come see my stand-up comedy

166
00:07:14,700 --> 00:07:16,666
'cause I had a little moment

167
00:07:16,766 --> 00:07:18,066
-of trying stand-up.
-Right.

168
00:07:18,366 --> 00:07:22,833
And I remember one manager saw a flyer
that I put up in the break room

169
00:07:23,576 --> 00:07:27,447
and she was like, "You do comedy?
I'd like to see that."

170
00:07:27,866 --> 00:07:28,933
And just walked out.

171
00:07:29,033 --> 00:07:32,166
Translation, "No way is that guy funny.
There's no way."

172
00:07:33,666 --> 00:07:38,200
I had found a stand-up comedy workshop,

173
00:07:38,299 --> 00:07:43,833
a junior college adult-ed,
like Irvine Valley extension course.

174
00:07:43,933 --> 00:07:45,833
And it was a six-week class.

175
00:07:45,933 --> 00:07:48,733
-"Learn how to be a stand-up comedian."
-Right.

176
00:07:48,799 --> 00:07:52,333
It was literally just a guy with
a Peavey amp and a microphone

177
00:07:52,400 --> 00:07:54,233
in a junior high classroom,

178
00:07:54,299 --> 00:07:55,933
and he would just stand up there.

179
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:58,400
In the first class were 30 people,

180
00:07:58,466 --> 00:08:03,166
and by the second class,
there were 15 people,

181
00:08:03,433 --> 00:08:05,299
and by the third class,
there were seven of us.

182
00:08:07,133 --> 00:08:08,566
Tell me how you came up...

183
00:08:08,666 --> 00:08:12,599
Did you have a cat? The cat bit
from your audition for SNL.

184
00:08:12,766 --> 00:08:13,833
No, I didn't have one.

185
00:08:13,933 --> 00:08:15,133
-Come on.
-Yeah.

186
00:08:15,200 --> 00:08:16,066
Girlfriend had one?

187
00:08:16,266 --> 00:08:18,599
-No, I just watched cats.
-Come on!

188
00:08:19,165 --> 00:08:20,566
[Will] That's a harrowing moment.

189
00:08:20,633 --> 00:08:23,933
We had to do our auditions at 8H,
you know, the studio

190
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:25,666
-where they do the show.
-Right.

191
00:08:25,733 --> 00:08:29,066
But he had you do it to an empty...
There's no one in... There's no audience.

192
00:08:29,166 --> 00:08:34,699
So, I'm rolling around playing with
cat toys to dead silence thinking,

193
00:08:35,033 --> 00:08:36,699
"This is the end. This is it."

194
00:08:36,933 --> 00:08:39,533
With just the television camera
and the sound guy...

195
00:08:40,586 --> 00:08:42,121
[giggling] Right.

196
00:08:42,766 --> 00:08:44,633
-...wondering when his lunch break is.
-Yeah.

197
00:08:44,699 --> 00:08:46,833
And I'm rolling around with cat toys.
But I thought,

198
00:08:47,366 --> 00:08:48,933
"There's no turning back now."

199
00:08:49,033 --> 00:08:51,633
Now, I'm watching this,
whatever years later, going...

200
00:08:51,733 --> 00:08:53,866
-Right.
-..."My God. You got him with the cat bit.

201
00:08:53,966 --> 00:08:56,699
This guy just became a star
'cause he had a cat bit."

202
00:08:57,066 --> 00:08:57,900
Thank you very much.

203
00:08:57,966 --> 00:08:59,600
[Will] I wrote that at the Groundlings.

204
00:08:59,666 --> 00:09:02,266
Just a guy who would go
over in the corner

205
00:09:02,366 --> 00:09:04,900
and play with cat toys
while we're having a dinner party.

206
00:09:05,144 --> 00:09:06,979
The other guests are like,
"What is he doing?"

207
00:09:07,066 --> 00:09:09,366
It's like, "It's just something he does.
Anyway, so,

208
00:09:09,433 --> 00:09:12,000
-we're trying to get Tommy into school--"
-That's really great.

209
00:09:12,066 --> 00:09:13,433
And I'd just be over there...

210
00:09:13,533 --> 00:09:15,266
Then I walk over to her
at a certain point.

211
00:09:15,333 --> 00:09:17,733
She'd bring the feather on the stick

212
00:09:18,366 --> 00:09:21,466
and she'd just keep talking
and I'd play with it. Yeah.

213
00:09:23,462 --> 00:09:24,864
[Jerry] We have a cat now.

214
00:09:24,933 --> 00:09:27,933
I gave it to my wife for Mother's Day
'cause I thought,

215
00:09:28,033 --> 00:09:30,500
-"One time in life--"
-Did she want a cat?

216
00:09:30,600 --> 00:09:33,466
She had mentioned it and I would say,
"Absolutely not. I don't want that."

217
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:37,900
And I thought, "One time in life, I wanna
give someone a box with a kitten in it."

218
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:40,066
-Right? One time.
-Sure.

219
00:09:40,133 --> 00:09:42,333
And she'd feel it
and she feels something moving around.

220
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:43,833
You know, and it's not a real box.

221
00:09:43,900 --> 00:09:48,066
You know, it's a TV type box.
It opens, and the cat jumped out.

222
00:09:48,133 --> 00:09:49,666
-And did it...
-It was fantastic.

223
00:09:49,733 --> 00:09:51,400
-Great.
-And this cat

224
00:09:51,466 --> 00:09:53,633
is fascinated by mechanical things.

225
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:56,395
So, the cat is constantly on the phone
in the house.

226
00:09:56,462 --> 00:09:58,564
You'd go in the room
and you see the phone--

227
00:09:58,633 --> 00:10:00,900
-A speaker phone?
-And then start playing with it,

228
00:10:00,967 --> 00:10:03,970
start hitting the buttons, and we'd joke,
"He's trying to reach another cat."

229
00:10:04,036 --> 00:10:05,104
[mimics dialing]

230
00:10:05,171 --> 00:10:08,474
And I say to him,
"Don't play with the phone anymore.

231
00:10:08,600 --> 00:10:10,133
Stop playing with the phone."

232
00:10:10,533 --> 00:10:12,699
-And does he even look at you?
-Yeah.

233
00:10:13,613 --> 00:10:16,549
Is there anything more futile
than telling a cat

234
00:10:16,616 --> 00:10:18,818
-what you don't want it to do?
-There's no-- Totally.

235
00:10:18,900 --> 00:10:21,000
-"Don't do that."
-It would never listen to you.

236
00:10:21,066 --> 00:10:22,699
His name is Javier.

237
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:27,066
I say, "Javi, don't do that
with the phone. It's annoying."

238
00:10:27,159 --> 00:10:28,527
And he probably just goes...

239
00:10:36,233 --> 00:10:37,600
"I gotta go right back to it."

240
00:10:37,666 --> 00:10:40,333
They will play with something,

241
